latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialize:\n   - Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n   - Initialize an empty list to store transformed L-shapes.\n\n2. Define helper functions:\n   a. is_valid_L_shape(grid, row, col, size): Checks if an L-shape of given size (3 or 5) exists at the specified position.\n   b. transform_L_shape(grid, row, col, size): Changes the specified L-shape from black (0) to green (3).\n\n3. Corner check:\n   - For each corner of the grid, check for L-shapes (both size 3 and 5).\n   - If found, transform the largest valid L-shape and add to the transformed list.\n\n4. Edge scan:\n   - Scan along each edge of the grid (top, right, bottom, left).\n   - For each black square on the edge, check for L-shapes (both sizes).\n   - Transform valid L-shapes, prioritizing larger ones and those that contribute to balance.\n   - Add transformed shapes to the list.\n\n5. Inward spiral:\n   - Start from just inside the edges and spiral inward.\n   - At each black square, check for L-shapes (both sizes).\n   - Transform valid L-shapes that improve overall balance.\n   - Add transformed shapes to the list.\n   - Continue until reaching the center or no more valid transformations are possible.\n\n6. Balance check:\n   - If the number of transformed shapes is less than 2, revisit the grid:\n     a. Look for smaller L-shapes or slightly relax the criteria.\n     b. Transform additional shapes if found, aiming for at least 2 total.\n\n7. Final pass:\n   - Make a final pass over the grid to ensure no obvious L-shapes were missed.\n   - Transform any remaining L-shapes that significantly improve balance.\n\n8. Return the transformed grid.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n1. For the inward spiral, use a layer-by-layer approach:\n   - Define a layer as a rectangle of cells just inside the previous layer.\n   - Process each layer from the outermost to the innermost.\n\n2. Balance consideration:\n   - Keep track of the center of mass of transformed shapes.\n   - When deciding whether to transform a new shape, consider how it affects this center of mass.\n   - Aim to keep the center of mass close to the grid's center.\n\n3. Prioritization:\n   - Always prefer extended L-shapes (size 5) over regular ones (size 3) if both are valid.\n   - When multiple L-shapes are possible, choose the one that best improves overall balance.\n\n4. Stopping criteria:\n   - Stop the main transformation process when reaching the center of the grid.\n   - Also stop if no new transformations have been made in a complete layer.\n\n5. Final check:\n   - After the main process, quickly scan the entire grid one last time.\n   - Transform any missed L-shapes only if they significantly improve balance or if the total number of transformations is still low.\n\nThis plan follows the observed patterns in the examples, prioritizes edge-to-center progression, allows for flexibility in the number of transformations, and focuses on achieving overall visual balance in the grid."
